<document xmlns="http://cnx.rice.edu/cnxml" xmlns:m="http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML">
  <title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</title>
  <metadata xmlns:md="http://cnx.rice.edu/mdml">
    <md:content-id>m02026</md:content-id>
    <md:title>7.16.1: Three Forms of Quadratic Expressions</md:title>
    <md:uuid>d64620ab-e0c8-4c44-9ad2-d33e00b8e630</md:uuid>
  </metadata>
  <content>
  <section><title>Warm Up Activity</title>
<para>Expressions in different forms can be used to define the same function. Here are three ways to define a function
  \(f\).</para>
<table class="os-raise-wideequaltable">
  <thead>
    <row>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Standard form</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Factored form</entry>
      <entry scope="col" class="emphasis">Vertex form</entry>
    </row>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=x^2-4x+3\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-3)(x-1)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=(x-2)^2-1\)
      </entry>
    </row>
    <row>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=ax^2+bx+c\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-r_1)(x-r_2)\)
      </entry>
      <entry>
        \(f(x)=a(x-h)^2+k\)
      </entry>
    </row>
  </tbody>
</table>

<para>Which form would you use if you want to find the following features of the graph of \(f\)? Be prepared to explain
  your reasoning.</para>
<div class="os-raise-ib-pset" data-button-text="Check" data-content-id="72134a6d-e1e2-4c16-81a2-e3a16765eb09" data-fire-learning-opportunity-event="eventnameY" data-fire-success-event="eventnameX" data-retry-limit="0" data-schema-version="1.0">

  &amp;ZeroWidthSpace;


  <!--Q#-->
  &amp;ZeroWidthSpace;
  <exercise data-content-id="9cf689e7-5f8c-4a48-ba74-2a2f83303671" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Factored form" data-solution-options="[&quot;Standard form&quot;, &quot;Factored form&quot;, &quot;Vertex form&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The \(x\)-intercepts</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Factored form. In factored form, we can see that the \(x\)-intercepts, or roots, are located at
        \((r_1, 0)\) and \((r_2, 0)\). </para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  <!--Q#2-->
  &amp;ZeroWidthSpace;
  <exercise data-content-id="380e1643-a11c-4cb6-8845-ec65a4da1a50" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Vertex form" data-solution-options="[&quot;Standard form&quot;, &quot;Factored form&quot;, &quot;Vertex form&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The vertex</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! Vertex form. In vertex form, we can see that the graph has a vertex located at the point defined by
        \((h, k)\). </para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
  &amp;ZeroWidthSpace;<!--Q#3-->
  &amp;ZeroWidthSpace;
  <exercise data-content-id="6e893041-f82f-43ab-a110-2237251bfb5d" data-problem-type="dropdown" data-solution="Standard form" data-solution-options="[&quot;Standard form&quot;, &quot;Factored form&quot;, &quot;Vertex form&quot;]">
    <problem>
      <para>The \(y\)-intercept</para>
      </problem>
    <solution>
      <para>Correct! In standard form, we can see that the graph intersects the \(y\)-axis at 3, but in any form, we can
        pretty easily find the \(y\)-intercept when the input is 0.</para>
    </solution>
    </exercise>
  <!--END QUESTION.-->
